# Faithful port of Sagan's Parse_IP() to VRL.
#
# Why this exists
# ---------------
# Sagan rules group correlations on internal values such as src_ip, which the
# engine derives by scanning the raw message rather than by reading a field.
# Sigma can only group on fields, so sagan2sigma refuses those rules unless the
# field exists. This transform creates it, which turns roughly 296 refused
# rules of the upstream corpus into working correlations.
#
# What it reproduces
# ------------------
# Source: src/parsers/ip.c in quadrantsec/sagan. The engine does NOT use a
# regular expression. It rewrites a fixed set of delimiters to spaces, splits
# on whitespace, and validates each token with inet_pton(). Position matters:
# `parse_src_ip: 2` means the second valid address in the message, so the
# ordered list is preserved rather than just the first hit.
#
# Deliberate fidelity details, each one observable in the engine source:
#
#   * the delimiter set is exactly the one at ip.c:120, so `src=1.2.3.4`,
#     `(1.2.3.4)` and `"1.2.3.4"` all yield a bare token, while `.`, `:` and
#     `#` survive because the address forms below need them;
#   * tokens equal to tcp, udp or icmp are consumed as protocol hints and never
#     treated as addresses;
#   * a token is only considered when it has at least two colons or at least
#     three dots, and at most four dots (ip.c:255), which is what rejects
#     version strings like 1.2.3 and 1.2.3.4.5;
#   * `1.2.3.4:8080`, `1.2.3.4#53`, `1.2.3.4.` and their IPv6 equivalents are
#     all accepted, matching the separate branches at ip.c:439, 476, 556, 644,
#     786 and 849;
#   * validation uses ip_to_ipv6(), whose accept/reject behaviour matches
#     inet_pton() including the rejection of leading zeros such as 01.2.3.4.
#
# Output
# ------
#   .sagan_ip_1 .. .sagan_ip_5   addresses in order of appearance
#   .src_ip                      alias for .sagan_ip_1
#   .dest_ip                     alias for .sagan_ip_2
#
# The aliases follow the corpus convention: 94% of parse_src_ip uses ask for
# position 1 and 91% of parse_dst_ip for position 2. Rules asking for another
# position are converted against .sagan_ip_N directly, so the aliases are a
# convenience and never a source of imprecision.

body = string(.message) ?? ""

# Sagan's delimiter rewrite, ip.c:120.
normalised = replace(body, r'["()\[\]<>{},/@=\-!|+_&%$~^\x27]', " ")

found = []

for_each(split(normalised, " ")) -> |_index, raw| {
    token = raw
    lowered = downcase(token)

    # Protocol keywords are consumed by the engine, never scanned as addresses.
    if lowered != "tcp" && lowered != "udp" && lowered != "icmp" && token != "" {
        dots = length(split(token, ".")) - 1
        colons = length(split(token, ":")) - 1
        hashes = length(split(token, "#")) - 1

        # ip.c:255. Anything outside this envelope cannot be an address.
        if (colons >= 2 || dots >= 3) && dots <= 4 {
            candidate = token

            # Trailing dot, as in "connection from 1.2.3.4." (ip.c:439, 786).
            if ends_with(candidate, ".") {
                candidate = slice!(candidate, 0, length(candidate) - 1)
                dots = dots - 1
            }

            # A separated token is checked on BOTH sides, left first, exactly as
            # the engine does with strtok_r (ip.c:476 and 556). This is what
            # makes "outside:203.0.113.7" and "1.2.3.4#53" both resolve: the
            # address sits after the separator in the first case and before it
            # in the second, and Sagan validates whichever half parses.
            halves = [candidate]
            if hashes == 1 {
                halves = split(candidate, "#")
            } else if colons == 1 && dots == 3 {
                halves = split(candidate, ":")
            }

            for_each(halves) -> |_position, half| {
                address, error = ip_to_ipv6(half)
                if error == null && address != null {
                    found = push(found, half)
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

if length(found) > 0 { .sagan_ip_1 = found[0] }
if length(found) > 1 { .sagan_ip_2 = found[1] }
if length(found) > 2 { .sagan_ip_3 = found[2] }
if length(found) > 3 { .sagan_ip_4 = found[3] }
if length(found) > 4 { .sagan_ip_5 = found[4] }

# Convenience aliases matching the dominant corpus convention.
if length(found) > 0 { .src_ip = found[0] }
if length(found) > 1 { .dest_ip = found[1] }
